<https://w3id.org/mailo> a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:abstract "MAILO (Medical AI Legal Ontology) is a machine-executable knowledge graph encoding EU regulatory obligations for medical artificial intelligence systems. It formalises eight EU legislative instruments -- the GDPR, MDR, AI Act, Product Liability Directive, EHDS, Data Governance Act, Data Act, and Trade Secrets Directive -- as OWL 2 classes and SHACL shape constraints, grounded in three CJEU judgments on automated decision-making (Ligue des droits humains, SCHUFA Holding, and Dun & Bradstreet Austria). MAILO functions as a deterministic, auditable compliance-verification layer: SHACL NodeShapes validate instance data describing a medical AI system against the encoded legal constraints and produce structured, traceable conformance reports."@en ;
    dcterms:created "2026-03-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:description """\r
        Ontology capturing the legal landscape governing AI-driven automated\r
        decision-making in medical contexts. Covers CJEU precedents on GDPR\r
        Art.22, UPC/EPO patent litigation in MedTech, the EU AI Act high-risk\r
        regime, MDR 2017/745, PLD 2024/2853, EHDS 2025/327, DGA 2022/868,\r
        and Data Act 2023/2854 — with full precedent chains, SHACL runtime\r
        constraints, and compliance obligation mappings.\r
        Imports: W3C DPV v2.0 (data privacy vocabulary), AIRO (AI risk ontology),\r
        ELI (European Legislation Identifier), ECLI (European Case Law Identifier).\r
    """@en ;
    dcterms:language "en" ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:title "Medical AI Legal Ontology (MAILO)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Round 5 (v5.1.0, pre-release): a systematic audit of all 48 LegalArticle individuals, rather than only previously flagged ones, found five mis-numbered citations. PLD Art.4 (Definitions) had been recorded as the strict-liability provision, which is Art.5, and PLD Art.7 (Defectiveness) had been recorded as the software-as-product definition, which is Art.4(1). DGA Art.2 (Definitions) had been recorded as the Chapter II re-use framework, which opens at Art.3. Data Act Art.15 (exceptional need) had been recorded as trade-secret protection, which is Art.4(6)-(8), and Art.17 (request procedure) as the B2G obligation, which is Art.14. Two cross-framework tension descriptions still carried article numbers superseded in Round 3. None of the five was detectable by any SHACL shape: each was a well-formed triple naming an article that exists. A PendingAmendment vocabulary was added so that proposed legislative change can be recorded without contaminating the in-force numbering, and is used to declare the Digital Omnibus proposal on Data Act Chapter V."@en ;
    owl:imports <http://data.europa.eu/eli/ontology>,
        <http://w3id.org/dpv>,
        <https://w3id.org/airo> .

mailo: a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:created "2026-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:creator "Victor Kao" ;
    dcterms:description "OWL ontology mapping EU regulatory frameworks (GDPR, AI Act, MDR, PLD, EHDS, DGA, Data Act) to medical AI automated decision-making, CJEU case law, UPC patent litigation, and compliance obligations." ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:publisher "KU Leuven, Faculty of Engineering Science" ;
    dcterms:title "Medical AI Legal Ontology (MAILO)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Version 5.0.2 (patch): corrected the ECLI recorded for C-203/22 (Dun & Bradstreet Austria) from the erroneous ECLI:EU:C:2025:145 to the correct ECLI:EU:C:2025:117."@en,
        "Version 5.1.0: corrective release consolidating four rounds of work, none previously deployed. (1) All nine CJEU paragraph citations were re-checked and all nine corrected; two substantive mischaracterisations were removed (the health-status exclusion is categorical under PNR Directive Art.6(4), not curable by a GDPR Art.9(2) basis; the strict-necessity test does not entail invalidity, the Court having upheld the Directive by Charter-conforming interpretation). (2) The patent, EPO and UPC instances were verified: T 1191/19 re-dated and its holding rewritten, T 0161/18 corrected to a refused application, the citesPrecedent graph repaired, litigation statuses refreshed. (3) Legislative citations were audited: all four EHDS articles carried proposal numbering and were renumbered, Art.73 was added, PLD Art.11 was corrected from the causation presumption to exemption from liability, DGA Art.31 was corrected to Art.29, and AI Act Art.50 was relabelled. (4) MAILO's boundaries were made explicit: every case now declares an InclusionBasis and every principle an EncodingBasis, separating what a court held from what the ontology designer inferred or operationalised. Correcting the hierarchy that let the boundary errors pass, AutomatedDecisionSystem is no longer a subclass of MedicalAISystem — the entailment ran backwards, making every automated decision system medical. Parts IV and V of the SHACL shapes now enforce citation integrity and boundary declaration on the graph itself, and each shape has been tested against the error it was written for."@en ;
    owl:priorVersion <https://w3id.org/mailo/5.0.2> ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://w3id.org/mailo/5.1.0> ;
    owl:versionInfo "5.1.0" .

